1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,741
If they need a decoder ring to understand your offer, it is not an offer. It is a riddle. A one-page offer is a decision tool, not a brochure.

2
00:00:11,141 --> 00:00:20,030
Five parts. Everything else is optional. These five are not. Outcome. Scope. Deadline. Price. And some form of proof, even if it is tiny.

3
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:29,319
Outcome. What changes for them? Not the activities you will perform. What they will have, see, or stop worrying about when you are finished.

4
00:00:29,719 --> 00:00:39,348
Scope. What is included and what is not. Boundaries protect both sides. I will do X. I will not do Y or Z. Clear is kind.

5
00:00:39,748 --> 00:00:51,230
Deadline and price. When they get it and how much it costs. Specific delivery date or turnaround window. One clear number or a simple range that explains what moves the price.

6
00:00:51,630 --> 00:01:03,111
Proof. Even a tiny scrap beats nothing. A before and after, a named similar result, a short process note, or simply I have done this exact thing for three other teams.

7
00:01:03,511 --> 00:01:13,881
Format. One page. Readable on a phone. Big outcome at the top. Then the remaining pieces. Call to action at the bottom. No walls of text. No jargon.

8
00:01:14,281 --> 00:01:27,244
Your next move. Write one for a real problem today. Pick a service you can actually deliver. Fill the five slots. Send it to one real person. The fancy deck can stay in the drawer.

